## Scoring Guidance for Interviewers

| Signal | Strong candidate | Weak candidate |
|---|---|---|
| **Depth** | Explains *why* and the trade-offs, cites failures | Lists features and service names |
| **Specificity** | Names versions, limits, commands, real numbers | Speaks in generalities |
| **Currency** | Mentions Workload Identity, OIDC federation, Gateway API, GitOps, supply-chain signing | Relies on retired or legacy patterns (kubenet, PSP, pod identity, PAT/secret-based service connections) |
| **Debugging** | Layered, hypothesis-driven | Random command guessing |
| **Ownership** | Owns mistakes, describes systemic fixes | Blames tools or teammates |
| **Communication** | Adjusts explanation for the audience | Jargon-heavy or unable to simplify |

**Suggested interview structure (about 90 minutes):** Pick 2 questions from Tier 2, 2 from Tier 3, and 1-2 from Tier 4. Add Q21 or Q26 for behavioral signal. Use the probes liberally. Candidates who can defend their answers under follow-up are the ones who have actually done the work.

I can also turn this into a Word or PDF handout, add a hands-on lab exercise (for example, a broken AKS manifest to debug live), or tailor it to a specific role such as SRE or platform engineering.
